import Mathlib

/-!
# What is the standard groupoid model of the Cuntz algebra?

Source: https://mathoverflow.net/questions/448788
Credit: @adammcarter

The question asks for the standard groupoid model of the Cuntz algebras `𝒪ₙ`, `n = 1, 2, …, ∞`:
an étale groupoid `G` whose groupoid C⋆-algebra is `𝒪ₙ`. The source points at Renault's
*A groupoid approach to C⋆-algebras* (LNM 793, 1980); the Cuntz groupoids are in Section III.2 there.
We state that standard answer:
`𝒪ₙ` is the C⋆-algebra of the Cuntz groupoid, the Deaconu–Renault groupoid of the one-sided shift
on the space of words in `n` letters (for `n = ∞`: the boundary path groupoid of the graph with one
vertex and countably many loops). References: Renault 1980; Deaconu, *Groupoids associated with
endomorphisms*, Trans. AMS 347 (1995) 1779–1786; Kumjian, Pask, Raeburn, Renault, *Graphs, groupoids,
and Cuntz–Krieger algebras*, JFA 144 (1997) 505–541 (finite `n`); Brownlowe, Carlsen, Whittaker,
*Graph algebras and orbit equivalence*, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 37 (2017), Section 2.3 (any
graph, so also `n = ∞`); Sims, *Hausdorff étale groupoids and their C⋆-algebras*, in Operator
Algebras and Dynamics (Adv. Courses Math. CRM Barcelona, Birkhäuser, 2020), arXiv:1710.10897,
Examples 2.1.16, 2.3.7, 2.4.7.

## The groupoid `G_ι` (alphabet `ι`, with `ι = Fin n` for `1 ≤ n < ∞` and `ι = ℕ` for `n = ∞`)
* Unit space `X_ι`: the words over `ι`, written as sequences `w : ℕ → Option ι` where `none` means
  the word has ended (once ended, it stays ended). For finite `ι` only infinite words are allowed;
  for infinite `ι` the finite words (including the empty one) are allowed too. This is the boundary
  path space of the graph with one vertex and `ι` loops: the vertex is an infinite receiver exactly
  when `ι` is infinite, and that is what makes `𝒪_∞` unital.
* Topology on `X_ι`: the one induced by the map sending a word to the set of its finite prefixes,
  viewed in the product space `List ι → Bool`. For finite `ι` this is the Cantor topology on `ι^ℕ`;
  for `ι = ℕ` it is the usual boundary path topology (cylinders `Z(μ)` minus finitely many `Z(μ i)`).
* Shift: `σᵐ w = (k ↦ w (k + m))`, defined on the words of length at least `m`.
* Arrows: triples `(x, m - n, y)` with `x` of length at least `m`, `y` of length at least `n`, and
  `σᵐ x = σⁿ y`. Product `(x, k, y) (y, l, z) = (x, k + l, z)`; source `(x, k, y) ↦ y`.
* Topology on arrows: generated by the sets `Z(U, m, n, V) = {(x, m - n, y) : x ∈ U, y ∈ V,
  σᵐ x = σⁿ y}` with `U`, `V` open, `U` inside the domain of `σᵐ` and `V` inside that of `σⁿ`
  (Brownlowe–Carlsen–Whittaker 2017, Section 2.3; cf. Renault, *Cuntz-like algebras*,
  arXiv:math/9905185, Section 2). It is finer than the subspace topology of `X × ℤ × X`.

## The groupoid C⋆-algebra
Mathlib has no groupoid C⋆-algebras, so we define the reduced one concretely. Each continuous
compactly supported `f : G → ℂ` acts on `ℓ²(G)` (counting measure on the arrows) by convolution,
`(f ⋆ ξ)(γ) = ∑_{α β = γ} f(α) ξ(β)`; this is the direct sum over the units `x` of the regular
representations on `ℓ²(G_x)`, so its operator norm is the reduced norm. We pin the operator by its
values on the point masses `δ_β`, and `C⋆_r(G)` is the closure of the star-subalgebra these operators
generate in the bounded operators on `ℓ²(G)`. The Cuntz groupoids are amenable (finite `n`: Renault 1980,
Section III.2; `n = ∞`: Paterson, *Graph inverse semigroups, groupoids and their C⋆-algebras*,
J. Operator Theory 48 (2002) 645–662, and Renault, *Cuntz-like algebras*, Section 2), so the full and
reduced C⋆-algebras coincide; the source does not distinguish them.

## The Cuntz algebra
`𝒪ₙ` is the universal C⋆-algebra generated by isometries `sᵢ` (`i ∈ ι`) with `sᵢ⋆ sⱼ = δᵢⱼ`, and
`∑ᵢ sᵢ sᵢ⋆ = 1` when `ι` is finite (for `n = 1` this gives `𝒪₁ = C(𝕋)`, the C⋆-algebra of one
unitary). Mathlib has no universal C⋆-algebras, so `IsGroupoidModel ι` says exactly that `C⋆_r(G_ι)`
has that universal property: it contains such a family `S`, generated by it as a C⋆-algebra, and every
such family `t` in any unital C⋆-algebra is the image of `S` under a unital ⋆-homomorphism. This
determines `C⋆_r(G_ι)` up to isomorphism as `𝒪ₙ`, for every `n` including `n = 1`. (The target
algebras range over `Type`; universe level plays no role here.)

## Reading of the source
The source asks for a description, so `main` states the standard description as a theorem: for every
`n = 1, 2, …` and for `n = ∞`, the C⋆-algebra of the Cuntz groupoid is `𝒪ₙ`. The literature request is
not a mathematical statement and is answered by the references above. The comment on the source
(`𝒪₂` as a crossed product of a Cantor set by `ℤ₂ ∗ ℤ₃`) is a different model for `n = 2` only.
-/

namespace OMA.S_mo_448788

open scoped ENNReal
open Classical

/-- Boundary words over the alphabet `ι`: `w k = none` means the word ended before position `k`.
Once ended it stays ended; finite words are allowed only when `ι` is infinite. -/
def BoundaryWord (ι : Type) : Type :=
  {w : ℕ → Option ι // (∀ k, w k = none → w (k + 1) = none) ∧ (Finite ι → ∀ k, w k ≠ none)}

/-- `prefixes w μ = true` exactly when the finite word `μ` is a prefix of `w`. -/
noncomputable def prefixes {ι : Type} (w : BoundaryWord ι) (μ : List ι) : Bool :=
  decide (∀ (i : ℕ) (h : i < μ.length), w.1 i = some (μ.get ⟨i, h⟩))

/-- The topology of the boundary path space: induced by the prefix map into `List ι → Bool`. -/
noncomputable instance (ι : Type) : TopologicalSpace (BoundaryWord ι) :=
  TopologicalSpace.induced prefixes inferInstance

/-- `w` has length at least `m`, so that the shift `σᵐ` is defined at `w`. -/
def LengthAtLeast {ι : Type} (w : BoundaryWord ι) (m : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ k, k < m → w.1 k ≠ none

/-- The Cuntz groupoid: triples `(x, m - n, y)` with `σᵐ x = σⁿ y`. -/
def CuntzGroupoid (ι : Type) : Type :=
  {g : BoundaryWord ι × ℤ × BoundaryWord ι //
    ∃ m n : ℕ, g.2.1 = (m : ℤ) - n ∧ LengthAtLeast g.1 m ∧ LengthAtLeast g.2.2 n ∧
      ∀ k, g.1.1 (k + m) = g.2.2.1 (k + n)}

/-- The basic open set `Z(U, m, n, V) = {(x, m - n, y) : x ∈ U, y ∈ V, σᵐ x = σⁿ y}`. -/
def cylinderPair {ι : Type} (U V : Set (BoundaryWord ι)) (m n : ℕ) : Set (CuntzGroupoid ι) :=
  {g | g.1.1 ∈ U ∧ g.1.2.2 ∈ V ∧ g.1.2.1 = (m : ℤ) - n ∧ ∀ k, g.1.1.1 (k + m) = g.1.2.2.1 (k + n)}

/-- The topology of the Cuntz groupoid, generated by the sets `Z(U, m, n, V)`. -/
instance (ι : Type) : TopologicalSpace (CuntzGroupoid ι) :=
  TopologicalSpace.generateFrom
    {Z | ∃ (U V : Set (BoundaryWord ι)) (m n : ℕ), IsOpen U ∧ IsOpen V ∧
      (∀ x ∈ U, LengthAtLeast x m) ∧ (∀ y ∈ V, LengthAtLeast y n) ∧ Z = cylinderPair U V m n}

/-- `ℓ²` of the arrows, with counting measure. -/
abbrev L2 (ι : Type) : Type := lp (fun _ : CuntzGroupoid ι => ℂ) 2

/-- `T` is the convolution operator of `f`: `T δ_β = ∑_{α} f(α) δ_{α β}`, that is, the `γ`-coordinate of
`T δ_β` is the sum of `f(α)` over the arrows `α` with `α β = γ`
(where `(x, k, y) (y, l, z) = (x, k + l, z)`). -/
def IsConvolutionOperator {ι : Type} (f : CompactlySupportedContinuousMap (CuntzGroupoid ι) ℂ)
    (T : L2 ι →L[ℂ] L2 ι) : Prop :=
  ∀ β γ : CuntzGroupoid ι,
    T (lp.single 2 β (1 : ℂ)) γ =
      ∑' α : {α : CuntzGroupoid ι //
          α.1.2.2 = β.1.1 ∧ (α.1.1, α.1.2.1 + β.1.2.1, β.1.2.2) = γ.1}, f α.1

/-- The reduced groupoid C⋆-algebra `C⋆_r(G_ι)` acting on `ℓ²(G_ι)`. -/
noncomputable def reducedAlgebra (ι : Type) : StarSubalgebra ℂ (L2 ι →L[ℂ] L2 ι) :=
  (StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ
    {T | ∃ f : CompactlySupportedContinuousMap (CuntzGroupoid ι) ℂ, IsConvolutionOperator f T}).topologicalClosure

/-- The Cuntz relations for a family indexed by `ι`: `sᵢ⋆ sⱼ = δᵢⱼ`, and `∑ sᵢ sᵢ⋆ = 1` if `ι` is finite. -/
def IsCuntzFamily {ι : Type} {A : Type*} [Ring A] [StarRing A] (s : ι → A) : Prop :=
  (∀ i j, star (s i) * s j = if i = j then 1 else 0) ∧ (Finite ι → ∑ᶠ i, s i * star (s i) = 1)

/-- `C⋆_r(G_ι)` is the Cuntz algebra: the universal C⋆-algebra of the Cuntz relations indexed by `ι`. -/
def IsGroupoidModel (ι : Type) : Prop :=
  ∃ S : ι → reducedAlgebra ι, IsCuntzFamily S ∧
    (StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range fun i => (S i : L2 ι →L[ℂ] L2 ι))).topologicalClosure =
      reducedAlgebra ι ∧
    ∀ (A : Type) [CStarAlgebra A] (t : ι → A), IsCuntzFamily t →
      ∃ φ : reducedAlgebra ι →⋆ₐ[ℂ] A, ∀ i, φ (S i) = t i

/-- The Cuntz algebras `𝒪ₙ`, `n = 1, 2, …` and `n = ∞`, are the C⋆-algebras of the Cuntz groupoids. -/
theorem main : (∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → IsGroupoidModel (Fin n)) ∧ IsGroupoidModel ℕ := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_mo_448788
